.Dd September 8, 2026
.Dt TREE 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm tree
.Nd display a tree of a directory's contents
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm tree
.Op Fl adfFgilnpqrstuxACDJQNU
.Op Fl L Ar level
.Op Fl o Ar file
.Op Fl P Ar pattern
.Op Fl I Ar pattern
.Op Fl Fl dirsfirst
.Op Fl Fl filelimit Ar n
.Op Fl Fl inodes
.Op Fl Fl noreport
.Op Fl Fl prune
.Op Fl Fl si
.Op Fl Fl sort Ns = Ns Ar name|size|mtime|ctime
.Op Fl Fl timefmt Ar fmt
.Op Fl Fl help
.Op Ar directory ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
recursively displays the contents of each
.Ar directory
in a tree-like format.
With no arguments,
the current directory is displayed.
If the argument is not a directory,
it is displayed alone.
.Pp
By default, the display is sorted alphabetically, and the tree is
drawn using box-drawing characters when the current locale uses the
UTF-8 codeset, and ASCII characters otherwise.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a
Include directory entries whose names begin with a dot
.Pq Sq \&. .
.It Fl d
Only show directories.
Symbolic links to directories are shown, but not followed.
.It Fl f
Show the full path of each entry.
.It Fl F
Append a slash
.Pq Sq /
to directories, an asterisk
.Pq Sq *
to executables, an equals sign
.Pq Sq =
to sockets, and a vertical bar
.Pq Sq \&|
to FIFOs.
For symbolic links, the marker of the link target is appended to the
target name instead.
.It Fl g
Displays the group name of the file or directory.
.It Fl h
Print the size of each entry in a more human readable way (e.g.\&
appending a size letter for kilobytes: 10K), using powers of 1024.
Implies
.Fl s .
.It Fl i
Do not print the indentation lines.
Useful when the output is consumed by scripts.
.It Fl l
Follow symbolic links if they point to directories, as if they were
directories.
Symbolic links that would result in recursion are displayed with the
message
.Sq [recursive, not followed] .
.It Fl L Ar level
Maximum display depth of the directory tree.
Entries at depth
.Ar level
are displayed but their contents are not.
.It Fl n
Turn colorization off.
.It Fl o Ar file
Send output to
.Ar file
instead of standard output.
.It Fl p
Print the protections for each file.
.It Fl P Ar pattern
List only those files that match the wildcard
.Ar pattern .
Directories are not affected.
.It Fl I Ar pattern
Do not list those files (or directories) that match the wildcard
.Ar pattern .
May be specified multiple times.
.It Fl q
Print non-printable characters in filenames as question marks instead
of the default octal escapes.
.It Fl Q
Quote the names of files in double quotes, using C-style escapes for
special characters.
.It Fl r
Sort the output in reverse order.
.It Fl s
Print the size of each file in bytes along with the name.
.It Fl t
Sort the output by last modification time instead of alphabetically.
.It Fl u
Print the username, or UID number if no username is available, of the
file.
.It Fl x
Stay on the current file system only, like
.Xr find 1 Fl xdev .
.It Fl C
Turn colorization on, using ANSI escape sequences.
.It Fl D
Print the date of the last modification time for the file listed.
.It Fl J
Prints out an JSON representation of the tree.
.It Fl N
Print non-printable characters as is instead of the default octal
escapes.
.It Fl U
Do not sort.
Lists files in directory order.
.It Fl Fl dirsfirst
List directories before files.
.It Fl Fl filelimit Ar n
Do not descend directories that contain more than
.Ar n
entries.
.It Fl Fl inodes
Prints the inode number of the file or directory.
Implies
.Fl s .
.It Fl Fl noreport
Omits printing of the file and directory report at the end of the
tree listing.
.It Fl Fl prune
Makes
.Nm
prune empty directories from the output, useful when used in
conjunction with
.Fl P
or
.Fl I .
Has no effect with
.Fl d .
.It Fl Fl si
Like
.Fl h ,
but use powers of 1000 instead of 1024.
Implies
.Fl s .
.It Fl Fl sort Ns = Ns Ar type
Sort the output by
.Ar type
instead of name.
Possible values are:
.Cm name ,
.Cm size ,
.Cm mtime ,
.Cm ctime .
.It Fl Fl timefmt Ar fmt
Use
.Ar fmt
(a
.Xr strftime 3
format) for the date output; implies
.Fl D .
.It Fl Fl help
Print usage information and exit.
.El
.Sh COMPATIBILITY
.Nm
aims for practical compatibility with the tree utility in common use
today (version 2.x of the original implementation).
Known differences:
.Bl -bullet
.It
The
.Fl P
and
.Fl I
patterns use
.Xr fnmatch 3
semantics; the
.Sq \&|
alternation and
.Sq ^
negation extensions of the original matcher are not supported.
.It
Patterns are matched against entry names and symbolic link targets,
but not against full paths of nested entries.
.It
The options
.Fl X
(XML),
.Fl H
(HTML),
.Fl R ,
.Fl A ,
.Fl S ,
.Fl v ,
.Fl Fl du ,
.Fl Fl charset ,
.Fl Fl matchdirs
and
.Fl Fl fromfile
are not implemented.
.It
The behaviour of the
.Fl s
option differs from the historical
.Nm :
it now prints file sizes like the original utility does.
Use
.Fl Fl noreport
to suppress the trailing report instead.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr find 1 ,
.Xr ls 1 ,
.Xr stat 2 ,
.Xr strftime 3
